{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head b9603b428c04c80f7a8f2e31ed6e778218974c54, artifact 69b5d11b at internal/kernel, under request d3f05938 and ratified changes-requested 9e9833d9.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD IS. One file, six added lines, internal/kernel/kernel_test.go: a new case in the TestCheckpointMetadataBindsEnvelopeTrailersAndTreeIndependently table. The commit carries exactly one Rests-On trailer, naming request d3f05938, which is right for assigned work. My checkout, the branch and origin all stand at the same commit; git diff --check is clean and the tree is clean before and after every probe below.\n\nTHE MUTATION PROOF HOLDS. Disabling only the guard at internal/kernel/checkpoint.go:541 turns exactly one subtest red:\n\n  --- FAIL: .../target_names_checkpoint_genesis\n      kernel_test.go:3151: checkpoint metadata mutation error = \u003cnil\u003e, want \"target does not name checkpoint genesis\"\n\nNil, not a different error. Nothing else in the package moves. Restoring the guard returns the package green and the tree clean.\n\nI ALSO CHECKED THE CASE CANNOT PASS FOR THE WRONG REASON, because a mutation case that trips an earlier guard proves nothing about the one it names. Dropping just the envelope rebuild line makes the same case fail with \"event 0 cached intent differs from sequence commit\" instead. So every line of the mutate body is load-bearing: re-signing keeps the signature valid, and rebuilding the envelope keeps the cached intent coherent, so the target guard is the first and only thing left to reject the input. The harness asserts strings.Contains on the guard's own message, so the case cannot be satisfied by any other refusal.\n\nSECURITY. No defect, and the boundary is real rather than nominal. verifySignedTarget at internal/kernel/kernel.go:1426 returns (decoded, decoded.Target == target, nil): a foreign target is not an error, only a false boolean. The caller's explicit branch is the sole thing that converts a validly signed event from another workroom into a refusal, so deleting that branch loses cross-workroom binding with no compile error. A stored checkpoint is untrusted input -- the neighbouring TestTruncatedCheckpointCannotPoisonSubmitterDedup already treats it that way -- so without this case the fast path would accept an event signed for a different genesis and present it as verified history. The failure mode is a refusal naming its reason; no secret, bound or signature path changes.\n\nI CHECKED THE PARALLEL PLACE, since the same helper has a second caller. internal/kernel/kernel.go:1387 carries the twin guard on the full-scan path, and I mutation-tested it too: it is already pinned, by TestVerifierRejectsIntentReboundToAnotherLog. So the fast path was the only unpinned side of the pair, which is exactly what the request claimed, and this head closes the asymmetry. No follow-up request is needed.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Layer 2, the kernel, and only its test surface. No non-test file changes, so no layer contract changes and docs/reference/architecture.md correctly does not move at this head. The case asserts a structural binding and reads no application meaning, so the semantic-free property holds.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut, and I mean that as a measured claim rather than a courtesy: the probe above shows the shortest form that still proves the guard is the form that is here. The case reuses the sibling cases' helpers and shape and carries no scaffolding of its own.\n\nGATES I RAN, at this head, stated exactly. go vet ./...: clean. go test -count=1 ./...: pass, 29 packages, no failures -- the internal/docset failure that blocked 9e9833d9 is gone at this base, which was the whole ask of that verdict. go test -race -count=1 ./...: pass, 29 packages, no failures; planner deferred this to the rebased head and I ran it here rather than approve against a gate the conditions mandate and nobody had run. Focused test: pass. git diff --check: clean.\n\nONE NOTE FOR THE MERGE, not a blocker. Main moved to 155926d3 after this head was cut, by a notes-only merge of mine that adds a single file under notes/ and cannot interact with internal/kernel. gs merge refuses a candidate that differs from the approved head, not one that sits behind main, so there is no need to re-cut. The live shipped artifact at internal/kernel is your own 205332e3 at cbf1ede7, which is on main, so the retirement this merge performs is same-author and needs no co-signature from me. I did not name it in this verdict's artifact set because it does not stand at the reviewed head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:69b5d11b3d666db2d190747851e42b22d0ee8c3f","head":"b9603b428c04c80f7a8f2e31ed6e778218974c54","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3160d3597a2d43d1ccc9efbce1f35749e2b18f36, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4ed0039d1801c047a5e9879318bda66827c06f83, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:69f14295b19b6e2eb9986ff8411eb111a4cd3798, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8ee6083509ae6913ffd4e3351db8f92a3719b22c","verdict":"approved"}}